# Attempt 2: Fedora — Why I Left

I have no complaints about Fedora. It was one of the distros I used for
months and never got bored of. Because of Fedora, I became more comfortable
not only with the terminal, but with Linux in general. I started using the
terminal for things like downloading software with `winget`, taking notes in
plain text files, moving files around, searching between things, and
updating the distro itself through the terminal. I also got properly
introduced to tools like Vim and nano, which I'd previously only seen through
TryHackMe rooms and YouTube videos. Fedora would definitely be a distro I'd
use for everyday work, as well as career-focused tasks.

But I had to leave Fedora because of a mistake I made while working on a
module at university.

### How it started

During my Computer Forensics class, we had to download **Safeblock**, a
Windows-based software write blocker developed by ForensicSoft and used in
digital forensics to prevent accidental modification of evidence on storage
media. It set the SSD I'd installed it on to read-only mode, which caused
various boot issues on the Windows side: the boot logo stayed on screen for a
long time, it restarted constantly, and it would freeze completely once it
reached the lock screen.

Regardless of the corruption Safeblock caused, Fedora worked perfectly
throughout. I couldn't find a single fault in it, and every program ran
smoothly. But I very quickly noticed that Fedora also wasn't able to open the
SSD. Trying to view its contents brought up a pop-up saying something like
"You don't have privileges to view or change this folder." That was how I
first found out something had gone wrong. I didn't focus on it much at the
time, keeping it in mind while prioritising an assignment that was due in a
couple of weeks.

### Discovering the real damage

Once I'd finished the research for that assignment, I switched to Windows to
open **Autopsy** (a free, open-source digital forensics platform and
graphical interface for The Sleuth Kit, used to examine disk images, storage
drives, and digital artifacts). That's when I was met with the lock screen
freezes and being stuck on the Windows logo for a long time.

After multiple hard resets, searching online for similar cases, and asking
Claude and ChatGPT for possible fixes, I finally got into the Windows side,
only to find I couldn't open any files on the Terra SSD. Every attempt
brought up a pop-up along the lines of "This file and multiple others are
corrupted."

### First attempts at a fix

I tried opening Command Prompt as administrator, but it was corrupted too. I
then opened PowerShell and ran a scan with `sfc /scannow`, which fixed the
Command Prompt problem, but I still couldn't access the drive or its
contents.

I spent the whole day trying to fix it, but with my assignment due in a
couple of weeks, I decided to deal with the deeper problem later. For the
moment, I'd remove the blocked drive, hoping that would stop the boot
corruption and let me focus on my work without too much trouble. Just to be
safe, I searched "will removing a drive from a laptop cause the dual boot to
be damaged" and found that it wouldn't, so I didn't hesitate to remove it.

### When it got worse

Once I removed the drive, the Windows side got worse, not better: it was no
longer able to boot to the lock screen at all. I reinstalled the SSD, hoping
that would let it boot properly again, and tried several terminal commands
to fix it, but nothing worked.

As a last resort, I went back to the restore point I'd originally set up for
the dual boot. That actually worked: Windows stopped freezing and started
working properly again, though it still didn't fix the blocked SSD itself.

### Running out of options

Over the following days, I kept troubleshooting through the terminal, asked
AI agents for help, got advice from a connection on LinkedIn, contacted
Safeblock support, and eventually got a professor to look into it (and got
scolded for it). Nothing worked. With time running out, I had no choice but
to reset the Windows side completely, which would fix not just the
corrupted, blocked drive, but also make the system a bit faster overall.

### Afterward

Once Windows was fully reset, I focused on the assignment for a couple of
days and finished it in time, recording a video as part of the submission. I
passed with 70+ marks and genuinely enjoyed the process.

When I logged back into the Fedora side, I found I still couldn't access the
SSD, which ruined my original goal: using it as a bridge to share files
between Windows and Linux, switching to whichever I needed without redoing
work or opening a new application just to move a file. I was still able to
use Fedora normally otherwise, but I also noticed the limited space I'd
originally partitioned for it was starting to affect performance, since I
now had to download ISO images separately inside Fedora as well.

I didn't lose hope. I decided to treat it as an opportunity to properly
finish documenting the journey for both Omarchy and Fedora, and to finally
start fresh with something similar to Fedora that gave me everything I
needed.

As much as I liked Fedora, I know that putting myself in uncomfortable,
unfamiliar territory is where I grow and learn the most. I also wanted to
document my journey properly this time, so I used this problem as an
opportunity to actually document a project the right way: showing what I
did, how I did it, and what problems came up along the way.
